This is part two of [A History of Coffee](/coffee/guides/coffee-history/). In [part one](/coffee/guides/coffee-history-beginnings/), coffee was a drink for Sufi devotions in Yemen. In the 1500s it got out into the world.

## Spreading with the pilgrims

Yemen sat on the routes to Mecca, and pilgrims on the Hajj came from all over the Islamic world. They tried coffee, liked it, and took it home. Traders carried it up the Red Sea to Egypt and on to Syria. By the early 1500s, it wasn't just a religious drink. People were drinking it in public, together, for fun.

## The first ban: Mecca, 1511

In 1511, **Khair Beg**, the governor of Mecca, didn't like what he saw: men gathering to drink coffee, gossip, play games and, he suspected, criticize him. He gathered religious scholars, got a ruling that coffee was harmful, and banned it. Supplies were burned.

It didn't last. The Mamluk sultan in Cairo, who outranked him (and reportedly enjoyed coffee), overturned the ban. This would become a pattern: coffee gets banned, people keep drinking it, and the ban quietly goes away.

## Coffeehouses appear

By the 1530s, **coffeehouses** were open in Cairo and Damascus. This was something new. Before them, there weren't many places where men from different backgrounds could sit together in public, talk, and stay a while without drinking alcohol. Coffeehouses filled that gap. People came for music, storytelling, chess and backgammon, and the news.

## Constantinople, 1554

In 1554, two men from Aleppo and Damascus opened the first coffeehouses in **Constantinople** (today's Istanbul), the capital of the Ottoman Empire. They caught on fast, and became known as "schools of the wise" for the poets, scholars and officials who gathered in them.

The Ottomans also gave us the style of coffee still known as Turkish coffee: very finely ground, simmered with water (and often sugar) in a small pot called a _cezve_, and served with the grounds settled in the cup.

## More bans, same result

Coffeehouses were places where people talked about the government, and governments noticed. Through the 1500s and 1600s, religious authorities and sultans tried to shut them down several times. The harshest crackdown came under **Sultan Murad IV** in the 1630s, who banned coffeehouses (along with tobacco and wine) and reportedly patrolled the city in disguise to enforce it.

None of the bans stuck for long. Coffee was too popular, and coffeehouses paid taxes.

## Guarding the supply

All of this coffee still came from Yemen through Mocha, and the trade was worth protecting. It's often said that beans were boiled or roasted before export so they couldn't be planted elsewhere. Whether or not that's true, Yemen kept its near-monopoly until the late 1600s, when a few smuggled seeds changed everything. That story is in [part six](/coffee/guides/coffee-history-goes-global/).

